// Regression fixture for final-review Critical 1 + Critical 2 (memory-
// audit-4e whole-branch review, probe P1): a global `text` pushed into a
// global `list of text`. Before the fix, `cl_free_globals` had no runtime-
// aliasing analysis for globals at all -- it froze `p1Gt`'s handle into
// `p1Gl`'s single element via a plain handle copy, then freed BOTH `p1Gt`
// and (via `p1Gl`'s own element-free loop) the same handle again: SIGSEGV
// at process exit. Now `p1Gt` is disqualified (bare-ident push-argument
// handoff, an existing rule newly reaching globals) and `p1Gl` is
// disqualified too (Critical 2's new rule: a push whose value fails
// lowEscapeFreshInit disqualifies the receiving container, so `p1Gl`'s own
// element-free loop never runs either). Both leak instead of double-
// freeing. Expected strict-mode leak count: 4 (see ../p1_push_global.leaks)
// -- `p1Gt`'s text (struct box + data handle, 2 blocks) plus `p1Gl`'s list
// (struct box + backing-array handle, 2 blocks); the one text handle they
// share in common is counted once (it's never actually freed, so it shows
// up as `p1Gt`'s leak, not doubled by `p1Gl`'s).

var p1Gt: text = "hello"
var p1Gl: list of text

func runP1PushGlobal() {
    p1Gl.push(p1Gt)
    alert("p1 done")
}
